%S A030019 1,1,1,4,29,311,4447,79745,1722681,43578820,1264185051,41381702275,
%T A030019 1509114454597,60681141052273,2667370764248023,127258109992533616,
%U A030019 6549338612837162225,361680134713529977507,21333858798449021030515,1338681172839439064846881
%N A030019 Number of labeled spanning trees in the complete hypergraph on n vertices (all hyperedges having cardinality 2 or greater).
%C A030019 Equivalently, this is the number of "hypertrees" on n labeled nodes, i.e. connected hypergraphs that have no cycles, assuming that each edge contains at least two vertices. - _Don Knuth_, Jan 26 2008. See A134954 for hyperforests.
%C A030019 Also number of labeled connected graphs where every block is a complete graph (cf. A035053).
%C A030019 Let H = (V,E) be the complete hypergraph on N labeled vertices (all edges having cardinality 2 or greater). Let e in E and K = |e|. Then the number of distinct spanning trees of H that contain edge e is g(N,K) = K * E[X_N^{N-K}] / N and the K=1 case gives this sequence. Clearly there is some deep structural connection between spanning trees in hypergraphs and Poisson moments.
%C A030019 From _Natalia L. Skirrow_, Aug 28 2026: (Start)
%C A030019 a(n+1) is also the number of partitions of [n] into an S, where an S is a set of U's, and a U is a nonempty set of (node attached to S)'s; there are A052888(n) partitions of [n] into a U. (The e.g.f. formula defining S(x) and U(x) in terms of each other follows from this; see also Example.)
%C A030019 We will refer to hyperedges as edges.
%C A030019 In a rooted hypertree, each edge is 'oriented', with a single member node labeled as its parent, such that each nonroot node is the parent in all but one edge.
%C A030019 U's are forests of rooted hypertrees (equivalently, hypertrees whose root is an unoriented edge that other edges are oriented towards), and S's are rooted hypertrees whose root node has been severed and removed from all edges.
%C A030019 If an n+1-th node is added to become the root, and every undirected edge gains it as a parent, there are n+1 nodes (including itself) to swap the root with, forming different rooted trees, so each S corresponds with an equivalence class of n+1 rooted trees.
%C A030019 The rooted trees can also be grouped into different equivalence classes of n+1 each, by reorienting the edges to point towards a new root while keeping the nodes in place. These equivalence classes are unrooted hypertrees, so this formulation is equivalent to the title definition/Knuth's comment. (End)
%D A030019 Warren D. Smith and David Warme, Paper in preparation, 2002.
%H A030019 Alois P. Heinz, <a href="/A030019/b030019.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..370</a> (first 101 terms from T. D. Noe)
%H A030019 Ayomikun Adeniran and Catherine Yan, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.07814">Gončarov Polynomials in Partition Lattices and Exponential Families</a>, arXiv:1907.07814 [math.CO], 2019.
%H A030019 Roland Bacher, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1102.2708">On the enumeration of labelled hypertrees and of labelled bipartite trees</a>, arXiv:1102.2708 [math.CO], 2011.
%H A030019 Maryam Bahrani and Jérémie Lumbroso, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1608.01465">Enumerations, Forbidden Subgraph Characterizations, and the Split-Decomposition</a>, arXiv:1608.01465 [math.CO], 2016.
%H A030019 INRIA Algorithms Project, <a href="http://ecs.inria.fr/services/structure?nbr=810">Encyclopedia of Combinatorial Structures 810</a>.
%H A030019 Louis H. Kalikow, <a href="https://people.brandeis.edu/~gessel/homepage/students/kalikowthesis.pdf">Enumeration of parking functions, allowable permutation pairs, and labeled trees</a>, PhD thesis, Brandeis University, 1999.
%H A030019 R. Lorentz, S. Tringali, and C.H. Yan, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.04039">Generalized Goncarov polynomials</a>, arXiv preprint arXiv:1511.04039, 2015.
%H A030019 Adam Piggott, <a href="https://admjournal.luguniv.edu.ua/index.php/adm/article/view/724">The symmetries of McCullough-Miller space</a>, Algebra and Discrete Mathematics 14(2) (2012), 239-266. [<a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20171109082007/http://www.facstaff.bucknell.edu/ap030/researchfiles/TheSymmetriesOfMMSpace.pdf">2011 preprint link</a>]
%H A030019 Natalia L. Skirrow, <a href="/A030019/a030019.pdf">a short proof of Vladimir Kruchinin's comment on A052888</a> (extended comment), 2026.
%H A030019 D. M. Warme, <a href="https://doi.org/10.18130/V3ZG4B">Spanning Trees in Hypergraphs with Applications to Steiner Trees</a>, PhD thesis, University of Virginia, 1998, Table 5.1.
%H A030019 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/AbelsBinomialTheorem.html">Abel's binomial theorem</a>.
%H A030019 <a href="/index/Tra#trees">Index entries for sequences related to trees</a>
%F A030019 a(n) = A035051(n)/n for n > 0.
%F A030019 a(n) = Sum_{i=0...n-1} Stirling2(n-1, i) n^(i-1), n >= 1. (Warme, Corollary 3.15.1, p. 59)
%F A030019 a(n) = E[X_n^{n-1}] / n, n >= 1, where X_n is a Poisson random variable with mean n.
%F A030019 1 = Sum_{n>=0} a(n+1) * x^n/n! * exp( -(n+1)*(exp(x)-1) ). - _Paul D. Hanna_, Jun 11 2011
%F A030019 E.g.f. satisfies: A(x) = Sum_{n>=0} exp(n*x*A(x)-1)/n! = Sum_{n>=0} a(n+1)*x^n/n!. - _Paul D. Hanna_, Sep 25 2011
%F A030019 Dobinski-type formula: a(n) = (1/e^n) * Sum_{k=0..oo} n^(k-1)*k^(n-1)/k!. Cf. A052888. For a refinement of this sequence see A210587. - _Peter Bala_, Apr 05 2012
%F A030019 a(n) ~ n^(n-2) / (sqrt(1+LambertW(1)) * (LambertW(1))^(n-1) * exp((2-1/LambertW(1))*n)). - _Vaclav Kotesovec_, Jul 26 2014
%F A030019 From _Natalia L. Skirrow_, Aug 28 2026: (Start)
%F A030019 a(n) ~ n^(n-2) * exp((W(1)+1/W(1)-2)*n) * W(1)/sqrt(1+W(1)), where W(1)+1/W(1)-2 = 2*(cosh(W(1))-1) = Integral_{x=0..1} W(x) dx = A332915-2 = 0.33036612...
%F A030019 Let T_n(x) denote the Touchard polynomial Sum_{k=0..n} Stirling2(n,k)*x^k.
%F A030019 a(n+1) = T_n(n+1)/(n+1).
%F A030019 E.g.f. of a(n+1): S(x) = exp(U(x)), where U(x) = exp(x*S(x))-1 is e.g.f. of A052888(n) (= T_n(n)/n).
%F A030019 a(n+1) = Sum_{k=1..n} C(n-1,k-1)*A052888(k)*a(n+1-k).
%F A030019 A052888(n) = n*a(n) + Sum_{k=1..n-1} C(n-1,k-1)*k*a(k)*A052888(n-k) for n>0.
%F A030019 a(n+1) = Sum_{k=0..n} C(n-1,k-1) * T_{n-k}(n) * Bell(k).
%F A030019 Sum_{k=0..n} (-1)^(n-k) * A399383(n,k) * a(k+1) = 1. [Cf. _Vladimir Kruchinin_'s comment on A052888, and Skirrow, 2026 link for generalisation.]
%F A030019 T_n(c)/c = Sum_{k=0..n} C(n,k) * T_{n-k}(-k) * T_k(k+c)/(k+c), an analogue of Abel's binomial theorem. Specialises to:
%F A030019 Bell(n) = Sum_{k=0..n} C(n,k) * T_{n-k}(-k) * a(k+1).
%F A030019 a(n+1) = Sum_{k=0..n} C(n,k) * T_{n-k}(-k) * T_k(k+n+1)/(k+n+1).
%F A030019 (End)
%e A030019 From _Natalia L. Skirrow_, Aug 28 2026: (Start)
%e A030019 Here, S and U objects have curly and round brackets respectively to distinguish them, though they are both unordered.
%e A030019 Only a single representative of each conjugacy class is shown.
%e A030019 a(0+1) = 1:
%e A030019  {}.
%e A030019 a(1+1) = 1:
%e A030019  {(1->{})}.
%e A030019 a(2+1) = 4=2+1+1:
%e A030019  {(1->{(2->{})})} has 2 relabelings:
%e A030019  {(1->{}, 2->{})},
%e A030019  {(1->{}), (2->{})}.
%e A030019 a(3+1) = 29=6+3+3+6+1+6+3+1:
%e A030019  {(1->{(2->{(3->{})})})} has 6 reps,
%e A030019  {(1->{(2->{}, 3->{})})} has 3 reps (1, 2<->3),
%e A030019  {(1->{(2->{}), (3->{})})} has 3 reps (1, 2<->3),
%e A030019  {(1->{}, 2->{(3->{})})} has 6 reps,
%e A030019  {(1->{}, 2->{}, 3->{})},
%e A030019  {(1->{}), (2->{(3->{})})} has 6 reps,
%e A030019  {(1->{}), (2->{}, 3->{})} has 3 reps (1, 2<->3),
%e A030019  {(1->{}), (2->{}), (3->{})}. (End)
%t A030019 a[n_] := Sum[ StirlingS2[n-1, i]*n^(i-1), {i, 0, n-1}]; a[0] = 1; Table[a[n], {n, 0, 18}](* _Jean-François Alcover_, Sep 12 2012, from 2nd formula *)
%t A030019 (* Alternative: *)
%t A030019 n=18; CoefficientList[Series[Exp[InverseSeries[Series[Log[1+a]/Exp[a], {a, 0, n}], x]], {x, 0, n}], x]*Range[0, n]! (* _Natalia L. Skirrow_, Aug 28 2026 *)
%o A030019 (PARI) {a(n)=if(n==0,1,(n-1)!*polcoeff(1-sum(k=0, n-2, a(k+1)*x^k/k!*exp(-(k+1)*(exp(x+O(x^n))-1))), n-1))} /* _Paul D. Hanna_ */
%o A030019 (PARI) /* E.g.f. of sequence shifted left one place: */
%o A030019 {a(n)=local(A=1+x); for(i=1, n, A=exp(-1)*sum(m=0, 2*n+10, exp(m*x*A+x*O(x^n))/m!)); round(n!*polcoeff(A, n))} /* _Paul D. Hanna_ */
%o A030019 (Python)
%o A030019 from functools import cache
%o A030019 A052888=cache(lambda n: n and (c:=1)*n*A030019(n) + sum((c:=c*(n-k)//k)*(n-k)*A030019(n-k)*A052888(k) for k in range(1,n)))
%o A030019 A030019=cache(lambda n: int(n<2) or (c:=1)*A030019(n-1) + sum((c:=c*(n-k)//(k-1))*A030019(n-k)*A052888(k) for k in range(2,n))) # _Natalia L. Skirrow_, Aug 28 2026
